redistribution obligation, and scripts/verify_toolkit.py fails without it.

================================================================================
WHAT THIS LICENCE ASKS OF YOU
================================================================================

Using the toolkit:      nothing. GPL-3.0 attaches obligations when you convey
                        (distribute) the software, not when you run or modify
                        it privately.

Its output:             yours. GPL-3.0 covers the program, not what it
                        produces. Sprites, audio, models, GDScript written
                        through these servers carry no obligation -- licence
                        your game however you like.

Distributing a fork:    release the complete corresponding source under
                        GPL-3.0, keep the notices and every LICENSE file, state
                        what you changed, and add no further restrictions.

The Godot addon:        servers/godot/addons/godot_mcp/ is meant to be copied
                        into your own Godot project, which puts GPL-3.0 files
                        in a tree you may want to keep proprietary. It is an
                        EditorPlugin: it runs in the editor, not in your game,
                        and Godot does not load addons/ in an exported build.
                        A shipped build containing none of its code conveys
                        nothing, so no obligation arises for your game. If you
                        do ship a build that bundles it, the obligations above
                        apply to that code.

Only servers/blockbench/ forces GPL-3.0. Remove it and its toolkit.json entry
and the rest (MIT, MIT, Apache-2.0) would permit a permissive licence again.

